 "memo_md": "# Draft for the Planning Commission: RM-M interior side setback 10 → 4 ft\n\n> **What-if, not the law.** **Drafted by an AI agent; check before sending.**\n\n**Question.** What if RM-M's interior side setback were 4 ft instead of 10?\n\n**The text that controls it.** §903.03.C (data/code/ch903.txt, the saved Pittsburgh Code):\n\n> Minimum Interior Sideyard Setback | | R1D, R2 & R3 Subdistricts | 5 ft. | R1A Subdistrict | 5 ft. | RM Subdistrict | 10 ft.\n\n**Redline.** Strike ~~10 ft.~~, insert **4 ft.** · The minimum interior sideyard setback for the RM Subdistrict under Moderate Density site development standards would decrease from 10 ft. to 4 ft.\n\n**Count.** The app's citywide classifier (scripts/build-scenarios.ts, the code behind the rule what-ifs), with only this change, over the districts whose rules a person has signed (R1D-H, RM-M, R2-L, R1D-L, R2-H, R1D-M):\n- **two-unit house:** 135 City-owned vacant lots that don't fit the dimensional rules today would (87 listed for sale; 18 still pencil). By district: RM-M 135. Most in: Lincoln-Lemington-Belmar 40, Homewood South 33, Homewood North 24, Middle Hill 18, Crawford-Roberts 6.\n- **three-unit house:** 24 City-owned vacant lots that don't fit the dimensional rules today would (17 listed for sale; 6 still pencil). By district: RM-M 24. Most in: Homewood South 10, Middle Hill 6, Lincoln-Lemington-Belmar 3, East Hills 2, Homewood North 1.\n\n**Caveats.**\n- A what-if, not the law: changing the code takes City Council. The count is City-owned vacant lots only, dimensional rules only, sale status aside.\n- This calculation does not show how many parcels or existing structures would become newly compliant under the reduced setback standard.\n- This count does not reflect potential site-specific impacts on fire safety, building code separation distances, or neighboring privacy.\n\n_Planned and drafted by gemini-3.6-flash · 24×100 policy agent · Q4. Check every quote against the code before sending._\n",
